1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
Did you know there's more than one dark galaxy?

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:11,600
Last year, China's FAST telescope spotted FAST J01399P4328, another hydrogen cloud with

3
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,560
almost no stars.

4
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:17,479
It's just over a million light years away, shaped like an S.

5
00:00:17,479 --> 00:00:24,639
Its total mass is around 5 billion suns, but the stars make up less than 700,000 suns.

6
00:00:24,639 --> 00:00:28,520
Ordinary matter is just 1 and 50th of its mass.

7
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:31,040
In other words, it's a dark matter monster.

8
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:34,279
Astronomers even have a name for these faint beasts.

9
00:00:34,279 --> 00:00:36,040
Low surface brightness galaxies.

10
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:41,959
They're so dim, even their brightest parts are darker than our night sky.

11
00:00:41,959 --> 00:00:43,959
Most are tiny dwarfs.

12
00:00:43,959 --> 00:00:49,360
Once a cloud tops a billion solar masses, it usually fires up star formation, except

13
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:50,919
these rebels.

14
00:00:50,919 --> 00:00:52,319
Why care?

15
00:00:52,319 --> 00:00:56,159
Because dark galaxies might solve the missing satellites problem.

16
00:00:56,159 --> 00:00:59,419
We expect hundreds of small companions around the Milky Way.

17
00:00:59,419 --> 00:01:01,040
We only see a dozen.

18
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:04,120
Could most be hidden, starless clouds?

19
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:06,919
These dark dwarfs are local fossils.

20
00:01:06,919 --> 00:01:09,959
Galaxies frozen in time since the early universe.

21
00:01:09,959 --> 00:01:13,019
Crazy to think we're living next to these ancient relics.

22
00:01:13,019 --> 00:01:15,040
Share this if you want more space secrets.

